zed to a/b by pathlib ✓. And user = 'a//b' → a/b ✓. And user = './a' → a ✓. \n\nHmm, one catch: on Windows flavor, `Path(root)/'a\\\\b'` splits on backslash — that's fine/desired there.\n\nBut: candidate = Path(root)/user when user ends with '/' e.g. 'a/' → Path('a/') → parts ('a',) so candidate root/a. That's fine (trailing slash irrelevant).\n\nWhat about `user = 'a/..'`? rejected.\n\nWhat about `user = 'a/../'`? rejected.\n\nNow, does `Path(root) / user` ever produce something NOT under root lexically, given user is relative and has no `..`? On POSIX, no. On Windows flavor with weird inputs — we reject drive/UNC/rooted. But what about a user path like `'a:b'` on Windows? `PureWindowsPath('a:b').drive` = 'a:' → we'd reject. Slightly conservative but fine.\n\nHmm wait, there's one more: what about a user path that is exactly `'.'` → parts empty → reject ✓. What about `'./.'` → parts empty → reject ✓.\n\nAlso, do we need to reject when user_path is just a symlink pointing to root itself (physically root)? Yes — resolved == root_real → reject.\n\nNow the physical check:\n\n```python\n    root_path = Path(root_str)\n    candidate = root_path / user\n    root_real = os.path.realpath(root_str)\n    cand_real = os.path.realpath(candidate)  # candidate is Path; realpath accepts\n    if not _is_within(cand_real, root_real):\n        raise UnsafePathError(\"resolves outside of root\")\n    if os.path.normcase(cand_real) == os.path.normcase(root_real):  # physically root itself\n        raise UnsafePathError(\"path resolves to the root itself\")\n    return candidate\n```\n\nHmm: `os.path.realpath(candidate)` where candidate is a Path → returns str ✓.\n\nBut wait: how do we compare within? `_is_within(child_str, parent_str)`:\n\n```python\ndef _is_within(path, root):\n    try:\n        common = os.path.commonpath([path, root])\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n    return common == root\n```\nHmm, commonpath normalizes and compares components case-sensitively; on